	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="annotation.text" rect="(-420, -160, 340, 130)">
STEP 1 — LOAD DATA
Load the BRFSS 2015 CSV file.
• File: diabetes_binary_5050split_health_indicators_BRFSS2015.csv
• 70,692 rows × 22 columns
• Set Diabetes_binary as the CLASS (Meta) variable
  so k-Means ignores it during clustering.
    </text>
		<text id="1" type="annotation.text" rect="(-220, -160, 340, 130)">
STEP 2 — SELECT FEATURES
Remove Diabetes_binary from Features.
Move it to Meta so clustering is truly
unsupervised. Use the remaining 21
features as input to k-Means.
    </text>
		<text id="2" type="annotation.text" rect="(-20, -160, 300, 110)">
STEP 3 — NORMALISE
Apply Z-score Normalisation.
Reason: k-Means uses Euclidean distance —
without scaling, BMI (10–60) dominates
over binary features (0/1).
    </text>
		<text id="3" type="annotation.text" rect="(180, -160, 340, 150)">
STEP 4 — K-MEANS
• Enable "Optimize k": tests k = 2 to 8
• Scoring: Silhouette score (higher = better)
• Init: k-means++ (avoids bad initialisations)
• 10 random restarts, 300 max iterations
Orange selects optimal k automatically.
    </text>
		<text id="4" type="annotation.text" rect="(180, 300, 340, 110)">
STEP 5 — SILHOUETTE PLOT
Displays per-sample silhouette scores.
Score range: -1 (wrong cluster) to +1
(well-separated). Values &gt; 0.3 indicate
meaningful cluster structure.
    </text>
		<text id="5" type="annotation.text" rect="(380, -160, 340, 110)">
STEP 6 — BOX PLOT
Inspect feature distributions per cluster.
Switch the attribute (BMI, GenHlth, Age,
HighBP) to profile each cluster's
clinical and lifestyle characteristics.
    </text>
		<text id="6" type="annotation.text" rect="(380, 300, 340, 110)">
STEP 7 — SCATTER PLOT
Use PCA components (PC1, PC2) on axes.
Colour by Cluster to visualise separation
in 2D. Overlap indicates soft boundaries
— expected with health survey data.
    </text>
		<text id="7" type="annotation.text" rect="(580, -160, 300, 110)">
STEP 8 — DATA TABLE
Browse all records with cluster labels.
Use the Sort column feature to find
dominant feature values per cluster.
    </text>
		<text id="8" type="annotation.text" rect="(580, 300, 300, 120)">
STEP 9 — DISTRIBUTIONS
Compare Diabetes_binary distribution
within each cluster. Cluster with highest
diabetes prevalence = HIGH RISK group.
This validates cluster clinical meaning.
    </text>
	</annotations>
